  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 148 PM CDT Thu Oct 15 2026\n\nPattern evolution will be the name of the game today and to end the \nwork-week, which in turn will result in a significant cool down. \n\nThe mid-upper level large scale pattern across CONUS is generally \nridge-trough-ridge, including a pair of troughs moving across \nportions of the central CONUS. The southern stream trough continues \nto come off the Colorado Front Range and has more recently become an \nopen wave. This has pushed a surface low over the CWA early this \nafternoon, too aiding in pushing majority of shower activity out of \nthe CWA. Its associated cold front has begun to move into NW \nMissouri and NE Kansas, and will begin to cool down the area. \nUnfortunately though, a good amount of low-level moisture remains \nbehind the front, and will allow dreary and overcast conditions to \ncontinue through much or all of the remaining day-time period. \nScattered showers to isolated general thunder will remain possible \nnear and ahead of this frontal passage as well. \n\nMore significant changes come to the area as the northern stream mid-\nlevel trough digs out of the Canadian Plains and into the Northern \nPlains. Along with its associated surface low, a secondary cold \nfront will drop through the area this evening and overnight. It will \ninclude a strong push of drier air and CAA, rapidly plunging the \narea back below seasonal norms for mid-October. Hi-res guidance, \nsuch as HRRR/RAP, have noted a period of enhanced wind gusts with \nthis push (strong subsidence), including run-to-run depictions of \ngusts into the low-40s mph between 01z-06z this evening. NBM did \ncome in notably lighter during this window, have blended in HRRR/RAP \nsolutions to signal the increasingly likely period of enhanced wind \ngusts. By Friday morning, the much cooler conditions will have \nsettled across the area with expected lows in the lower 40s for \nmost. Skies may initially be on the clear side, but an area of \nstratus may drop through as the core of the northern stream mid-\nlevel trough passes through the region. Breezy conditions will \ncontinue through the day Friday as well with NW to N winds around 20 \nmph and gusts 25 to 35 mph. Ongoing CAA will also limit warming \nthrough the day, with most forecast in the low 50s. Of note, did \ndrop Friday highs a couple degrees below initial NBM, in conjunction \nwith neighbors, to blend in ongoing colder hi-res guidance. Cloud \ncover extent and duration will ultimately be the determinant there. \nFriday night into Saturday morning, potential for the first freeze \nfor NW/N portions of the CWA as skies clear and winds go light to \ncalm across the area. Given the cooler trend in guidance, have \ncoordinated a Freeze Watch for portions of the area with western and \nnorthern neighbors. \n\nThrough the weekend, eastward departure of the mid-level trough will \nallow height rises and ridging to begin to spread across the central \nCONUS. This keeps the sensible weather abundantly quiet and on the \nseasonably cool to seasonable, but warming side. Expect temperatures \nto rise each day, yielding mid-upper 50s Saturday and mid-upper 60s \nSunday. \n\nThe start of the work week will see the weekend warming trend \npersist with continued southerly surface flow and gradually warming \n850mb temps. While mid-level flow will be more southwesterly in \nnature and a Baja California mid-level cutoff low gets absorbed into \nthe flow, precipitation chances remain minimal through at least mid-\nweek among the majority of members within ensemble guidance. \nPrevailing consensus remains for temperatures to rise back toward \nthe mid to upper 70s through mid-week.",
